# UI/UX implementation rule

Before editing a Flutter screen, inspect the existing target, `AppTheme`,
`AppPalette`, `Responsive`, shared widgets, and the relevant user journey.
Preserve the app's Material 3 system, Provider/Clean Architecture, Inter/Cairo
typography, dark/light themes, and English/Arabic RTL behavior.

- Reuse semantic tokens and shared components instead of local colors, spacing,
  or duplicate controls.
- Use `L10n`, logical directional APIs, semantics, accessible contrast, 48dp
  touch targets, and layouts resilient to text scaling, keyboards, and
  narrow/wide/foldable screens.
- Include loading, empty, error, offline, and long-content states in UI work.
- Keep motion purposeful and consistent with existing animation/transition
  helpers; avoid generic AI visual tropes and unapproved redesigns.
- Validate phone/wide, light/dark, and English/Arabic states where practical,
  then report anything not exercised.

Read the complete shared policy in `AGENTS.md` section 8 before a substantive
UI/UX task. Use the installed Dart/Flutter MCP and skills only when their
scope matches this established app; do not apply setup skills that would
replace its manual `L10n` or GetX navigation conventions.
